    "narrative": "Ohio's 11.8 million residents span an ideal demographic mix—family-dense neighborhoods and senior-rich communities across 88 counties—perfectly aligned with Hearth & Home's dual promotional strategy.\n\nOhio presents a uniquely favorable landscape for a value-priced family diner. With a total population of 11,810,293 spread across 88 geographic areas, the state offers both the density and the demographic diversity that Hearth & Home Family Diner is built to serve. The concept's twin promotional pillars—kids-eat-free night and senior discounts—map directly onto Ohio's age structure, where seniors (65+) range dramatically from 9.8% to 35.7% depending on the county. This spread means the state contains both youthful, family-heavy markets that respond to the kids-eat-free draw and mature communities where senior-discount messaging will resonate strongly. Few concepts are as naturally suited to serve both ends of this spectrum.\n\nThe economic fundamentals reinforce the fit. Median household income ranges from $42,500 to $73,603, squarely in the sweet spot for a $10–$15 per-person comfort-food menu. Value-priced dining is not merely tolerable in these communities—it is the natural choice for households managing everyday budgets. Labor force participation between 42.8% and 70.5% further signals where active working populations will drive reliable weekday lunch and evening traffic, while lower-participation, senior-weighted areas point toward off-peak daytime demand that pairs neatly with senior-discount hours.\n\nCommunity stability seals the case. Home-ownership rates from 71.1% up to 100% describe deeply rooted, settled neighborhoods—precisely the repeat-visit customers a neighborhood comfort-food spot depends on. Established, family-rooted households form the loyal customer base that sustains a local diner year after year. Together, Ohio's age diversity, budget-friendly income profile, and high residential stability create an environment where Hearth & Home can win on both value and community loyalty.",
